#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wf-chooser — a decision put to the user arrives as a chooser, or the turn does not stop.

WHAT WENT WRONG, and it is the whole specification. An `/agenda today` run posted 31
proposed items as plain markdown and asked for an answer in codes — "1-4 accept, 5-6
sbdc, 7-31 S". Six background employees then finished one after another, and every
hand-back produced another assistant turn that scrolled the list off the screen and
ended "still waiting on your decisions". The run sat for about fifteen minutes until the
user asked what was held up. Most of the 31 items were bulk decisions on dateless TODOs
that needed nobody.

THE USER'S WORDS ARE THE SPEC, 2026-09-24: "You shouldn't be waiting on my answer,
especially if it isn't absolutely evident that you need my input on something. ... If
there is an answer that it is needed, it needs to be evident in a chooser situation" —
"much like we choose the budget for audit" — "and this functionality needs baked into
the workforce process itself without org."

So there are three defects and one mechanism. A turn parked on input it did not need; it
parked in prose the user had to decode instead of in the harness's own chooser; and a
later turn narrated over the pending question instead of re-presenting it. `Stop` is the
only place all three are visible at once: the reply is written, the tool calls are
recorded, and the harness — not the turn — decides what happens next. `decision:
"block"` turns the reason into the next instruction.

WHY NOT `/org`. The user asked for this "without org", so it rides the user-scope
install: `wf-settings-apply --wire-defaults` registers it and `update` refreshes it, the
same path `wf-task-tag` takes. An org-dispatch rule reaches only projects somebody
re-audits.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It never judges whether the decision was worth asking — that is a
reading, and a hook has no model. It judges one mechanical fact: the reply parks on the
user, and this turn made no `AskUserQuestion` call. The remedy is in the reason.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PRECISION IS MEASURED, NEVER ASSERTED. A speech guard was retired from this
distribution at a 7.0% false-fire rate for teaching its reader to ignore it
(`changes/1.30.1.md`), and `wf-widen`'s two evidence triggers were held to 0.5% and 1.5%
before either shipped. Same bar here, same method: every turn under
`~/.claude/projects/` was walked, the reply that ENDED each turn was taken (the text
after that row's last tool call), turns that already called `AskUserQuestion` were
dropped, and each candidate class was counted against the total.

  CORPUS   1,539 transcripts across 38 project directories under `~/.claude/projects`,
           3,634 completed turns, this operator's real traffic, measured 2026-09-24.

  KEPT     codes     0.19%   (7 turns)    the reply asks for an answer in codes
           waiting   0.94%   (34 turns)   parked on the user's answer NOW
           options   0.19%   (7 turns)    a list the reply asks the user to choose from
           union     1.29%   (47 turns)   inside the 1.5% budget wf-widen is held to

  ALL 47 FIRES WERE READ BY HAND. 46 are a reply that puts a decision to the user in
  prose with no chooser — including four turns of the agenda run this was built from,
  each ending "I'm still waiting on your decisions for items 1-31". ONE is wrong: a
  sentence describing a design change ("TODOs without a date will stay without one by
  default instead of each needing your decision") is a report, not a park. That is a
  measured false-fire rate of 1 turn in 3,634 — 0.03%.

  DROPPED  offer     4.87%   (177 turns)  "want me to …?", "should I …?", "let me know"
           at the end of a reply. Over budget by more than three times, and the
           measurement says why it has to be: the same shape is `wf-widen`'s HANDOFF
           exemption, the one stop this distribution has already decided is CORRECT —
           the user's own rule is not to stop mid-work "unless you actually really do
           need input, your soliciting input from me." An offer IS that solicitation.
           Firing on it would block the honest hand-off along with the stall, which is
           precisely how the 7.0% guard earned its retirement. Not shipped.

  Four narrowings the measurement forced, each kept because dropping it cost precision
  rather than because it read better:
    · An instruction-only `codes` class ("reply with codes/letters/numbers") fired 3
      times and was wrong all 3 — "I'll have a real answer, with verify's own exit
      code" matches "answer … with … code". The verb has to land near an actual
      LEGEND (`1-4 accept`, `S = skip`) before it means anything. A legend ALONE is
      also noise: at 2.20% it caught this project's own canary reports, whose
      conclusion is the line `C=no-agent`.
    · `waiting` unnarrowed fired on 50 turns, and six of the fourteen read were a
      PROMISE TO ASK LATER — "when Order A lands I'll bring you one message with
      everything that needs your decision." That turn is working, not parked. A
      sentence carrying a first-person future marker is exempt, which is the same
      distinction `wf-widen`'s CONDITIONAL exemption draws for the promise trigger.
    · A quoted phrase is a label being reported, not a question being put: two fires
      were a turn saying it had left rows marked "needs your call".
    · `say which` had to be addressed to the reader. "The graphics review will … say
      which slide types need a clean diagram" is a sentence about a review.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

EXIT CODES: 0 always, and `stop_hook_active` returns immediately. A hook that can block
forever is a wedged session, and a wedged session is worse than the defect it watches.
"""

import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import sys

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))


def _sibling(name):
    """Share a sibling hook's transcript reader rather than write a second one.

    `wf-turn-ledger` owns where this stop's reply lives and how it is confirmed against
    the transcript, and a second copy is a second thing to keep in step — the copy that
    drifts is the one that fires wrong. NEVER WRITE BYTECODE: importing by path makes
    CPython drop a `__pycache__/` inside the SHIPPED directory, which `bin/check` reports
    as an undeclared file on the first run.
    """
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    modname = name.replace("-", "_")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(modname, os.path.join(HERE, name))
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(modname, loader))
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


TL = _sibling("wf-turn-ledger")

# THE TAIL ONLY, AND LONGER THAN `wf-widen`'S. A question asked in the middle of a long
# reply and answered by the reply itself is not this defect; the last words are where
# the parking happens. 600 rather than 400 because the shape this exists for is a long
# proposal list whose "reply with …" line sits under it — at 400 the agenda case's own
# instruction fell outside the window on one of the four transcripts that carry it.
TAIL = 600

# ── CLASS 1: THE REPLY ASKS FOR AN ANSWER IN CODES ───────────────────────────────
# A LEGEND is a token standing for a meaning: `1-4 accept`, `7-31 S`, `S = skip`. On its
# own it is ordinary prose (`C=no-agent` appears in this project's own canary reports),
# so it counts only next to a reply verb, or repeated — a pair of mappings in one clause
# is a key, and a key exists to be answered in.
_LEGEND = (r"\b\d{1,2}\s*[-–]\s*\d{1,2}\s+[A-Za-z]{1,12}\b"
           r"|(?<![\w-])[A-Za-z]{1,4}\s*=\s*[A-Za-z]")
CODES = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:reply|respond|answer|send back)\b[^\n]{0,120}?(?:" + _LEGEND + r")"
    r"|(?:" + _LEGEND + r")[^\n]{0,30}?,[^\n]{0,30}?(?:" + _LEGEND + r")", re.I)

# ── CLASS 2: THE TURN SAYS IT IS PARKED ON THE USER ──────────────────────────────
# The nouns are the user's own: a decision, an answer, a call. "Waiting on the gate" or
# "blocked on four background agents" is a different fact and is not here — that turn is
# waiting on a machine, and `wf-widen` already handles it.
WAITING = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:waiting|wait|held|holding|blocked|stalled|paused|parked|pending)\b[^.\n]{0,30}"
    r"\b(?:on|for)\b\s+(?:your|you)\b[^.\n]{0,30}"
    r"\b(?:decisions?|answers?|replies|reply|responses?|response|input|call|go-?ahead"
    r"|confirmation|sign-?offs?|choices?|choice|direction|verdict|approval)\b"
    r"|\b(?:need|needs|needing|await|awaiting)\b[^.\n]{0,20}\byour\b[^.\n]{0,20}"
    r"\b(?:decisions?|answers?|reply|response|input|call|go-?ahead|confirmation"
    r"|sign-?offs?|choices?|choice|direction|verdict|approval)\b"
    r"|\bover to you\b|\bball is in your court\b", re.I)
# A FIRST-PERSON FUTURE MARKER IN THE SAME SENTENCE MEANS THE ASK IS STILL TO COME.
# "When the order lands I'll bring you everything that needs your decision" describes a
# turn that is working; blocking it would push a question the turn has not formed yet.
LATER = re.compile(r"\bi\s?['’]?ll\b|\bi will\b|\bi'?\s?m going to\b"
                   r"|\bi am going to\b|\bi'?\s?ll be\b", re.I)
_SENTENCE = re.compile(r"(?<=[.!?:])\s+|\n+")

# ── CLASS 3: A LIST THE REPLY ASKS THE USER TO CHOOSE FROM ───────────────────────
# Both halves are required. A numbered list alone is how every report in this project is
# written; a choosing phrase alone is usually an offer, which is the class measured over
# budget and deliberately not shipped.
OPTION_LIST = re.compile(r"^\s{0,3}(?:\(?\d{1,2}[.)]|[-*]\s*\**[A-D][.):]|[A-D][.)])\s+\S", re.M)
# `pick` is bare nowhere here: "may pick up the 5.5 GB" and "pick them back up" were two
# of the four false fires in the unnarrowed class, both of them phrasal verbs.
CHOOSE = re.compile(
    r"\bwhich (?:one|option|of (?:these|those)|do you|would you)\b"
    r"|\bwhich\b[^.\n]{0,40}\bdo you want\b"
    r"|\b(?:pick|choose|select) (?:one|which|any|either|from|the one)\b"
    r"|\b(?:tell|let) me know which\b|\byour pick\b"
    # ADDRESSED TO THE READER, NOT NARRATED. "Say which, and I'll do it" is a chooser in
    # prose; "the review will judge every image and say which slide types need a
    # diagram" is a sentence about a review, and it was one of the measured false fires.
    r"|(?:^|[.\n:]\s*)(?:just |please |then )?say which\b"
    r"|\b(?:say|reply|respond)(?: back)? (?:the|a|with the|with a) number\b"
    r"|\boption \d\b", re.I)

CLASS_WHY = {
    "codes": "the reply asks for an answer in codes, which is a chooser rendered as "
             "homework: the user has to hold a key in their head, scroll back for the "
             "list, and type it out",
    "waiting": "the reply says it is parked on the user's answer, and nothing on screen "
               "is a question the harness will keep in front of them",
    "options": "the reply lists options and asks the user to choose from them in prose, "
               "which is exactly what AskUserQuestion renders",
}


def parked(reply):
    """The classes this reply trips, with the words that tripped each. [] when none."""
    tail = (reply or "")[-TAIL:]
    hits = []
    m = CODES.search(tail)
    if m:
        hits.append(("codes", m.group(0).strip()))
    # A QUOTED PHRASE IS A LABEL, NOT A PARK, and the mask is `wf-turn-ledger`'s own —
    # the same one the universal-claim trigger uses, for the same reason. MEASURED: two
    # of the 36 `waiting` fires were a turn REPORTING that it had left rows marked
    # “needs your call”, which is a finished piece of work being named, not a
    # question being put. Masking is applied here only: the `codes` class reads legends
    # that are written in backticks on purpose.
    for seg in _SENTENCE.split(TL._QUOTED.sub(lambda m: " " * len(m.group(0)), tail)):
        if LATER.search(seg):
            continue
        m = WAITING.search(seg)
        if m:
            hits.append(("waiting", m.group(0).strip()))
            break
    m = CHOOSE.search(tail)
    if m and OPTION_LIST.search(tail):
        hits.append(("options", m.group(0).strip()))
    return hits


def asked_with_a_chooser(payload):
    """True when this turn called `AskUserQuestion`.

    The question was put the way the user asked for it, so there is nothing to block —
    whatever the prose around it says. Walked from the transcript rather than inferred
    from the reply, because the call leaves a record and the prose does not.
    """
    lines = TL._read_lines(TL.reply_transcript(payload))
    if not lines:
        return False
    for line in lines[TL.current_turn(lines):]:
        try:
            d = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if not isinstance(d, dict) or d.get("type") != "assistant":
            continue
        for b in (d.get("message") or {}).get("content") or []:
            if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_use" \
                    and b.get("name") == "AskUserQuestion":
                return True
    return False


def reason(hits):
    """The block payload. The reason IS the fix, never "try harder".

    Three instructions in the order they have to happen: decide what needs no one,
    finish that work, and put whatever genuinely remains into the harness's own chooser.
    The caps are platform facts, not style — `AskUserQuestion` takes at most four options
    per question and four questions per call, and several calls in one message are
    presented NEWEST FIRST, so the user answers them backwards (`platform.md` fact 26).
    """
    lines = ["This turn stopped on a decision put to the user in plain text, and called "
             "`AskUserQuestion` nowhere in it.", ""]
    for cls, quote in hits:
        lines.append(f"  · {cls} — {CLASS_WHY[cls]}. It reads: “{quote}”")
    lines += [
        "",
        "Do these in order, in THIS turn:",
        "",
        "1. DECIDE EVERYTHING THAT DOES NOT NEED THE USER. Anything you could decide and "
        "reverse is yours to decide; say in one line what you decided and move on. The "
        "run this guard was built from put 31 items to the user, and most of them were "
        "bulk decisions on dateless items that needed nobody.",
        "2. FINISH THE WORK THAT DECISION UNBLOCKS, rather than reporting that it is "
        "pending. A partial result delivered beats a complete result withheld waiting "
        "for an answer.",
        "3. IF A GENUINE DECISION REMAINS — one that changes what gets built and that "
        "you cannot make — ASK IT NOW WITH `AskUserQuestion`, in this same turn. At "
        "most 4 options per question and 4 questions per call, and ONE call in the "
        "message: several calls in one message are shown newest-first, so the user "
        "answers them backwards. Never an option whose effect is to stop, and never "
        "permission for work already asked for.",
        "",
        "A question in prose scrolls away the moment anything else finishes. A chooser "
        "does not, which is the whole reason the user asked for one: “if there is an "
        "answer that it is needed, it needs to be evident in a chooser situation.”",
    ]
    return "\n".join(lines)


def chooser(payload):
    """The decision for this Stop, or None to say nothing at all."""
    # THE LOOP STOP, FIRST AND UNCONDITIONAL. This is a continuation of a turn already
    # blocked once; blocking it again is a session that can never end.
    if payload.get("stop_hook_active"):
        return None

    # A SUBAGENT HAS NO USER TO ASK. `AskUserQuestion` is not in a spawned employee's
    # tool set, so blocking its hand-back would demand a call it cannot make, forever.
    # Its hand-back is read by the MAIN turn, and the main turn's own Stop is where a
    # question buried in that report gets caught.
    if payload.get("hook_event_name") == "SubagentStop":
        return None

    reply = TL.confirmed_reply(payload)[0]
    # A REPLY THE TRANSCRIPT DOES NOT HOLD IS A SIDE QUERY, NOT THE END OF A TURN
    # (`wf-turn-ledger`, `confirmed_reply`). Nothing is judged: the turn is still going.
    if reply is None:
        return None

    hits = parked(reply)
    if not hits:
        return None

    # THE QUESTION WAS ASKED PROPERLY. Prose describing the chooser beside the chooser
    # is not the defect; the defect is the chooser's absence.
    if asked_with_a_chooser(payload):
        return {"systemMessage": "CHOOSER  a decision is pending and was asked with a chooser"}

    # THERE IS NO `background_tasks` EXEMPTION HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN
    # AN OMISSION. `wf-widen` exempts a turn with a task running because it judges
    # EVIDENCE, and a turn that is waiting has run nothing — judging it would block it
    # into doing work it was already waiting on. This hook judges no evidence. It judges
    # one fact about the reply, and the run it was built from is the counter-example the
    # exemption would have to survive: four of its turns say, in the measured corpus,
    # "Invest, research and YNAB are still running. I'm still waiting on your decisions
    # for items 1-31." Those turns were waiting on background rails AND on the user at
    # the same time, and it was the user's half that sat for fifteen minutes. A task that
    # does not involve the user never excuses parking on them, so the exemption is not
    # written; `chooser-background-task-and-parked-still-blocks` pins its absence.
    #
    # A BETWEEN-BATCHES TURN IS STILL SILENT, and structurally rather than by a guard:
    # the nouns in WAITING are the USER'S — your decision, your answer, your call — so
    # "I am waiting on the next batch before anything else moves" matches nothing here.
    # `chooser-between-batches-is-silent` pins that, against `wf-widen`'s own fixture text.
    return {
        "decision": "block",
        "reason": reason(hits),
        "systemMessage": ("CHOOSER  parked on the user in prose ("
                          + ", ".join(c for c, _ in hits)
                          + ") — decide what you can, then ASK WITH A CHOOSER"),
    }


def main():
    raw = sys.stdin.read()
    try:
        payload = json.loads(raw) if raw.strip() else {}
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return 0
    decided = chooser(payload)
    if decided:
        print(json.dumps(decided))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
